<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Francis Burton Harrison</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1873">1873</a>–<a href="/years/1957">1957</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Francis Burton Harrison was an American Democratic politician who served as Governor-General of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a> from 1913 to 1921 under President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/woodrow-wilson">Woodrow Wilson</a>. He rapidly replaced American officials with Filipinos and worked closely with Filipino leaders such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuel-l-quezon">Manuel Quezon</a>, preparing the islands for self-government. He later served as an adviser to President Quezon of the Philippine Commonwealth.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/francis-burton-harrison.jpg" alt="Francis Burton Harrison" width="320" height="439" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bain News Service, publisher · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Francis_Burton_Harrison.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Harrison was born in New York City to Burton Harrison, a lawyer who had been private secretary to the Confederate President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jefferson-davis">Jefferson Davis</a>, and the novelist Constance Cary Harrison. Through his mother he was a great-grandson of Thomas Fairfax, 9th Lord Fairfax of Cameron. He graduated from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yale-university">Yale College</a> in 1895 and from New York Law School in 1897, taught in its Evening Division, and served in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-american-war">Spanish–American War</a> as an assistant adjutant general with the rank of captain.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>A Democrat, he sat in the House of Representatives from 1903 to 1905, ran unsuccessfully for lieutenant governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> in 1904, and returned to Congress in 1907, resigning on 3 September 1913 to become governor-general of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a>. His Harrison Narcotics Tax Act was passed on 17 December 1914. While in the Far East he sought the Democratic presidential nomination of 1920, which went to James M. Cox.</p><p>In Manila Harrison oversaw Filipinization, the transfer of authority in the Insular Government to Filipinos in preparation for independence. The Jones Act, passed during his term, replaced the appointed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippine-commission">Philippine Commission</a> with an elected Philippine Senate and so gave the islands a fully elected legislature. He vetoed only five bills, the fewest of any American governor-general, which made him popular among Filipinos and a target of conservative Americans. In his last years, generous loans from the Philippine National Bank to agricultural cooperatives drained the colonial gold reserve.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After years in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, Harrison was recalled to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a> in 1934, and in November 1935 he became principal adviser to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuel-l-quezon">Manuel L. Quezon</a>, the first president of the Commonwealth. In 1936 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-assembly-of-france">National Assembly</a> made him a naturalized Filipino citizen by Commonwealth Act No. 79, and from May 1942 he again advised Quezon, now heading a government-in-exile. He later advised the first four presidents of the independent republic.</p><p>Harrison died on 21 November 1957 at Hunterdon Medical Center near Flemington, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-jersey">New Jersey</a>, and at his wish was buried in the Manila North Cemetery. He is the only former governor-general of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a> to have been granted Philippine citizenship, and streets in Manila, Pasay, Parañaque and Baguio bear his name.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Francis Burton Harrison” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Jones Act of 1916 created an all-Filipino legislature and promised independence, and Governor-General <b>Francis Burton Harrison</b> filled the civil service with Filipinos.</blockquote><a href="/state/insular-government-of-the-philippine-islands">Insular Government of the Philippine Islands</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in New York City, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. Died in Hunterdon Medical Center, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=40.71,-74.01&amp;year=1927" aria-label="New York City on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2736 710 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Francis Burton Harrison"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3186 980h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3186 980h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=40.71,-74.01&amp;year=1927">What was here in 1927? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
